{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790500441,"nonce":"d8mJzsZVyHT/B+QgGxJ9GcsJoFs=","title":"LICM: two pointer parameters of one function are taken to name different objects","message":"licm::MemLoc::may_alias treats distinct SSA roots as distinct objects\neverywhere but in an osr_region, where parameters are shared roots. A\nstore through one parameter and a load through another of the same\ntype then hoists the load, which is wrong when the caller passes one\nobject twice. Reproducer (wrong on the Mac debug build at e1567554):\n\n    class A:\n        def __init__(self, x):\n            self.x = x\n    def f(a, b, n):\n        s = 0\n        for i in range(n):\n            b.x = b.x + 1\n            s += a.x\n        return s\n    a = A(3)\n    print(f(a, a, 1000))      # CPython 503500\n\nZYNTAX_DUMP_HIR_DIR=\u003cdir\u003e ZYNTAX_BASELINE_THRESHOLD=1 zypy run t.py\nprints 3000: fn-f_trusted-body.hir has `load a.x` in the preheader.\nWithout the dump the run prints the right answer, so the body that\nruns then is optimised by another path; the fault is in the rule, and\nany tier that runs the full pipeline over such a body can hit it.\n\nThe sound rule is the brief's for C-licm-typed-alias: parameters,\nphis and loaded pointers are Unknown roots, which alias each other\nunless their pointee types are two different exact struct types\n(licm::MemLoc::distinct_exact_objects, landed in 9a2164e6). Changing\nit costs hoists wherever two parameters of one non-exact type meet a\nstore, so it needs an A/B of its own.","files":null}]}